From: Oliver Seiler <oseiler@unixg.ubc.ca>
Date: Thu, 27 Oct 1994 20:38:12 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-10-569] Anti-Statism Not Consistent with Boundless Expansion

First off I have to say that most (if not all) of the things that James brings
up in this posting have been argued to death, and really follow under "The
Basics". However, since it ended up in my mailbox, I'll make some comments
anyway (besides which, we seem to be having a lot of rehashed arguments coming
up here of late...)

On Wed, 26 Oct 1994, Kobielus, James wrote:

> Boundless expansion--throughout outer and inner space--seems to call for a 
> strong, enduring, well-heeled megastate, megacorporation, and/or 
> megareligion supported by all humanity.

Why this would be the case is of course unclear, since human beings have come
quite far without anything of the sort.

> OUTER SPACE:  Extropians have ambitious plans to probe, explore, colonize, 
> terraform, cultivate, and exploit all resources in the solar system for 
> human ends.  Realizing this goal would require a bureaucratic apparatus that 
> can enlist all nations, execute plans, mobilize resources, sponsor R&D, 
> construct monstruous public works, and launch and support interplanetary 
> missions over many generations.

What does bureaucracy have to do with boundless expansion. By definition,
nothing at all. Take something like nanotechnology. At this point I'd
have to say that most research in this area is being pursued at government
and corporate funded institutions. But this is just money. The real work is
being done by individuals putting some brain power into solving the problems
associated with nanotech. As in the semiconductor industry, only a few small
breakthroughs are necessary to realize nanotech as a product.

Look at NASA. This is an example of how little can be done when a lot of
money and people are thrown at ill defined goals, mostly paid for by the
tax-payer, who has no say in where that money goes. Commercialization of
space, when it happens, will grow quickly and without the need for
government help, nor for so called "mega" corporations (what is a mega-
corporation, by the way? still just people doing something. Paper pushing
will not push us to the frontiers of the galaxy anytime soon...)

> INNER SPACE:  Extropians desire to achieve physical/spiritual immortality 
> through cryogenics, cloning, and neural replication; elevate humanity to the 
> next evolutionary plateau through eugenics and mosaic conception/birthing; 
> overcome organic limitations through robotics and genetic engineering; 
> extend our minds and senses through information technology; and 
> microminiaturize the arts and sciences through nanotechnology.  Every one of 
> these goals would require considerable long-term, global R&D and would 
> inflict wrenching changes on ideologies, religions, legal systems, and codes 
> of interpersonal conduct everywhere.

So what's the problem? I don't believe that many religions will be adversely
affected. Hell, if most religions haven't disappeared by now, I don't think
what we do will change anybody if they don't want to. Not my concern anyway.
Legal systems? They *should* change. Ideologies, almost by definition, don't
really change much anyway. Things change, or things fall apart. This is
almost a law...

> Anybody who believes that these utopian goals can be achieved through pure 
> laissez-fair market mechanics without prodding/direction from central or 
> decentralized authorities should explain how.  None of this stuff will 
> spontaneously emerge from the primordial ooze.

It has been explained, in many places (read the required reading list...)
It has yet to be explained to me, aside from vague tales of "anarchy" and
"choas" which would ensue without government or authority, why government
is needed at all. How many times in a day do you actually come into
contact with the government? (Seriously, don't fall in to the trap that
roads wouldn't be built if people didn't want them. Or that we'd be all
screwed by the multiple phone companies which would exist in a free market.
Or that people would kill each other on the streets. These things happen
*now* and we do have a government...)

I was thinking on my way to work the other day how much like an anarchy (or
perhaps a libertarian society) driving in the city is like. There isn't
really any reason for people to follow the rules. In most cities, the
polive aren't generally evident anyway. People follow the rules because
it's in there better interests to cooperate with other drivers. The ones
who don't are the ones who get into trouble eventually...

> Every single Extropian dream requires massive infrastructure development and 
> a state apparatus unlike any in human experience.  Scattered ideamongers, 
> code cutters, and gene splicers won't make it all happen.

And guys sitting in offices, middle managers, supervisors, and other waste
organics won't do it for sure. The smart money is on the individuals coming
up with ideas.

> Jim Kobielus
> 

Sorry to piss on your party, but all you've done is tell us what you think,
but haven't supported it. Your ideas are appreciated, but really don't tell
us anything interesting or new. Nobody can argue with what you say, because
