Date: Fri, 28 Oct 1994 00:06:43 -0600
Subject: [#94-10-570] BABY: Reproduction

>John Schooley, writing to Nancy Lebovitz said:
>>Parents have been designing their offspring for a very long time
>>through mate selection.  [...] They needed to choose a genetically
>>superior [mate...]
>
>  As I understand genetics, until recently genes weren't visible (or
>even concieved of).  Choseing mates based on genotype was and is
>impractical.  We are still forced to base selections on phenotype, not
>genotype, for almost all features.  And, of course, there is no way to
>know before hand which genes will be (or even tend to be) successfull
>ones.
>
>  We can't today select the genes for financial success, running
>ability, or even nose size.  Although I understand that we do have a
>prety good handle on gender, and a few inherited disorders.  We can
>select for those mates who have a particular gender, financial
>success, running ability, or even nose size.  But this gives us only
>limited control over a baby's actual genotype or phenotype.
>
>j'

Point of course was:  what is changing is not principles -reproductive
rights- but the ways we can exercise them.  We are now wondering about the
right to choose physical traits of our offspring.  Being able to directly
access genotypes is on a direct continuum of being able to indirectly
access genotypes through phenotypes (a powerful technology in itself).  I
find the social resistance to making available and exercising the right-now
capability of manipulation of offspring traits through sperm banks a sign
of pervasive, archaic, strongly defended and therefore potentially
dangerous memes.  

The issue is not whether new technologies will eventually be applied - they
will- but how much destructive resistance will be put up against them and
who of us will suffer as a result.  It nice to gee whiz new technologies
but it is perhaps wiser to fit them onto the socially dominant and change
resistant memes rather than confronting them head on in cases where there
is no special need to do so.  (I have in mind an image of virus memes that
survive by taking charge of the meme codes or at least of symbiotic ones)
Your right to choose a husband is the same as my right to choose a sperm
bank donor.  Your right to choose a husband is the same as my right to do
gene correction for genetic disease on my newborn, or my right to enhance
the phenotype by altering the genotype in whatever way I choose.

It should be noted that reproductive rights are heavily biased towards the
woman, especially in societies where emancipation of women is becoming
actualized.  It is women who will at least in the medium term (until men
become emancipated from being just a convenient source of code) be in the
position to decide what sorts of reproductive options they care to pursue. 
They will eventually get their way because it will become recognized as an
issue of women's reproductive rights.  Forced interference by men into this
area will be recognized as a form of rapine, as well as an attempt to deny
women their basic human rights.

On an analogous level:  Your right to wear a push up bra is the same as my
right to have a breast implant.  Your right to have a face lift is the same
as my right to change my sex.  Your right to have a high IQ is the same as
my right to augment mine, etc.

