From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Thu, 27 Oct 1994 23:36:00 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-572] Generations by Strauss & Howe

I still think there's some merit in Strauss & Howe's theory of generational
types as presented in their first book, even if their latter book may be
trash.  As far as I'm concerned, the first chapters of their first book are
also trash, because they waste lots of time defending their methodology and
comparing theiry theory to other cyclical theories of history.  Boring!  The
interesting stuff is their interpretation of history according to their
theory - and also the most persuasive.  For instance, it makes a whole lot of
sense to classify the Boomers in the same category as the Puritan generation,
as well as the first and second Awakening generations (they call the second
Awakening generation the Transcendental generation) as well as the generation
of Gandhi, FDR, etc. (which they call the Missionary generation).  And it
also makes sense to put my generation (born: 1960-1982) in the same category
as the Lost generation, Mark Twain's generation, and the generation of Patrick
Henry and Thomas Paine.

The foundation of their theory is that there's a trend in parenting styles
from a very attentive, hands-on approach to an almost neglectful, laissez-faire
approach, and that this results in different characteristics in the generations
of kids that are raised those ways.  There's lots of foundation for the theory
that parenting style influences what a child will be like when he or she grows
up on an individual level.  They take it from the micro to the macro level,
with some success.

