From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Thu, 27 Oct 1994 23:28:19 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-573] REPRODUCTION: Hormonal Therapy

I was about to bring up the possibility of hormone therapy to engineer
desired results in offspring, when I saw that I'd been beaten to the punch
once again.  The one thing I'd like to add is that we know enough about
hormones and fetal development to influence the sex, height, sexual orien-
tation, aggressiveness, etc., of offspring - or, if we don't know exactly
how to do this yet, we will soon.  Presumably research with monkeys and
chimps will lead us to develop lots more knowledge.

So, the issue of "designer babies" is one that's a whole lot more immediate
than what it would be if we had to wait until we were capable of genetic
engineering.  The ethical questions about whether we should try to enhance
our babies through pre-natal hormone therapy are looming right over the
horizon, if not already with us.  For instance, it's theoretically possible
to make female fetuses into tomboys, which will tend to be be more like
males in some ways: better mathematical capability, more independent, more
aggressive and competitive, higher sex drive.  This sort of female would
make a very attractive mate to many men.  Should parents try to make their
female babies into tomboys?  Or not?  Why or why not?  Those are the sort
of questions we'll have to face, and very soon.

